/**
  @defgroup RodinVariational Variational module
  @brief Finite element spaces, form-language nodes, and variational problems.
 */

/**
  @defgroup TraitsSpecializations Traits Template Specializations
  @brief Specializations of Rodin::FormLanguage::Traits.

  Each specialization exposes the types a form-language node needs from its
  operands, including the scalar type, the range type, the finite element space and,
  for shape functions, the trial or test space they belong to.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin
  @brief The Rodin library for finite element methods and shape optimization.

  Rodin is a lightweight and modular C++20 finite element framework providing
  tools for solving partial differential equations and performing shape and
  topology optimization. Key modules include:

  - @ref Rodin::Geometry "Geometry" — Meshes, polytopes, and connectivity
  - @ref Rodin::Variational "Variational" — Finite element spaces, trial/test
    functions, and variational problem formulation
  - @ref Rodin::IO "IO" — Input/output in MFEM, MEDIT, HDF5, and XDMF formats
  - @ref Rodin::Solver "Solver" — Linear and iterative solvers (CG, GMRES, SparseLU)
  - @ref Rodin::Assembly "Assembly::OpenMP" — Assembly of stiffness matrices and load vectors
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Context
  @brief Execution contexts for local and distributed computation.

  @ref Rodin::Context::Local "Local" selects local execution, and @ref
  Rodin::Context::MPI "Context::MPI" carries the environment and communicator for distributed
  execution. Mesh, connectivity, and finite element specializations use these
  context types to select their data distribution and backend behavior while
  preserving the same mathematical interfaces.

  # Supported contexts
  - **Local Context**: Single-machine execution with shared memory
  - **Distributed Context**: Multi-node execution with message passing (MPI)

  # Representative use

  A mesh's context selects its execution environment without changing its
  geometric role.

  @code{.cpp}
  using LocalMesh = Geometry::Mesh<Context::Local>;
  auto mesh = LocalMesh::UniformGrid(Geometry::Polytope::Type::Triangle, {8, 8});
  @endcode

 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Alert
  @brief Contextual exceptions and diagnostic reporting.

  @code{.cpp}
  Alert::Info() << "Assembling the system..." << Alert::Raise;
  @endcode

  @ref Rodin::Alert::Exception "Alert::Exception" and @ref
  Rodin::Alert::MemberFunctionException "Alert::MemberFunctionException" report errors with
  contextual diagnostics. @ref Rodin::Alert::Info "Alert::Info" and @ref
  Rodin::Alert::Warning "Alert::Warning" provide nonfatal messages through the same stream
  interface. These classes communicate invalid inputs, unavailable operations, and
  solver or mesh-processing status.

  # Alert types

  | Type | Color | Behavior |
  |------|-------|----------|
  | @ref Rodin::Alert::Info "Alert::Info" | Blue | Informational message (non-fatal) |
  | @ref Rodin::Alert::Warning "Alert::Warning" | Yellow | Warning message (non-fatal) |
  | @ref Rodin::Alert::Success "Alert::Success" | Green | Success notification (non-fatal) |
  | @ref Rodin::Alert::Exception "Alert::Exception" | Red | Throws an exception (fatal) |
  | @ref Rodin::Alert::MemberFunctionException "Alert::MemberFunctionException" | Red | Exception with class/method context |
  | @ref Rodin::Alert::MemberFunctionWarning "Alert::MemberFunctionWarning" | Yellow | Warning with class/method context |

  # Usage pattern

  All alerts use the stream insertion operator `<<` and terminate with
  `Alert::Raise`.

  @code{.cpp}
  // Informational message
  Alert::Info() << "Assembling the system..." << Alert::Raise;

  // Warning
  Alert::Warning() << "Convergence is slow." << Alert::Raise;

  // Fatal exception (replaces throw)
  Alert::MemberFunctionException(*this, __func__)
    << "Incidence " << d << " -> " << dp << " has not been computed."
    << Alert::Raise;
  @endcode

  @warning Never use raw `throw` in Rodin code. Always use the Alert stream
  pattern with `<< Alert::Raise`.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Geometry
  @brief Meshes, geometric transformations, and mapped evaluation.

  @f[
    \mathbf{x}=F_K(\widehat{\mathbf{x}}).
  @f]

  @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Mesh<Context::MPI>" and @ref Rodin::Geometry::Connectivity
  "getConnectivity().compute(d1, d2)" describe mesh entities and their incidence relations. @ref
  Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Polytope", @ref Rodin::Geometry::Point "Geometry::Point", and
  @ref Rodin::Geometry::PolytopeTransformation "PolytopeTransformation" connect
  reference elements to physical geometry for integration, field evaluation, mesh
  extraction, and deformation.

  Key classes include:

  - @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Mesh<Context::MPI>" — The primary mesh class supporting
    triangular, quadrilateral, tetrahedral, and hexahedral meshes
  - @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Polytope" — Geometric entities (vertices,
    edges, faces, cells) with type enumeration
  - @ref Rodin::Geometry::Connectivity "getConnectivity().compute(d1, d2)" — Incidence relations
    between polytopes of different dimensions
  - @ref Rodin::Geometry::Point "Geometry::Point" — Evaluation point for grid functions,
    bundling reference coordinates, physical coordinates, Jacobian data, and
    the owning polytope

  # Mesh Creation

  | Method | Description |
  |--------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Mesh::UniformGrid(type, shape)" | Structured grid on the unit hypercube |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Mesh::Box(faceType, shape)" | Surface mesh (boundary of unit box) |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Mesh::Build()" | Programmatic construction via Builder pattern |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "mesh.load(file, format)" | Load from MFEM, MEDIT, or HDF5 |

  # Mesh Operations

  | Method | Description |
  |--------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Connectivity "getConnectivity().compute(d1, d2)" | Compute incidence relation @f$ d_1 \to d_2 @f$ |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "scale(factor)" | Scale all vertex coordinates by a scalar factor |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "displace(vectorFunction)" | Apply a displacement field to all vertices |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "skin()" | Extract the boundary surface mesh |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getDimension()" | Topological dimension (2 for triangles, 3 for tetrahedra) |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getSpaceDimension()" | Ambient space dimension (>= topological dimension) |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getVertexCount()" | Number of vertices |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getCellCount()" | Number of cells |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getVolume()" | Sum of measures of the three-dimensional entities |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getArea()" | Sum of measures of the two-dimensional entities |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getPerimeter()" | Total perimeter (or surface area) of the domain boundary |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getVertex() / getCell() / getBoundary()" | Polytope iterators |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "getVertexCoordinates(idx)" | Get coordinates of a vertex |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "setAttribute({dim, idx}, attr)" | Set attribute of a polytope (for BC labeling) |

  # Polytope types

  | Type | Dimension | Description |
  |------|-----------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Point" | 0 | Vertex |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Segment" | 1 | Edge |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Triangle" | 2 | 3-vertex face/cell |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Quadrilateral" | 2 | 4-vertex face/cell |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Tetrahedron" | 3 | 4-vertex cell |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Hexahedron" | 3 | 8-vertex cell |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Wedge" | 3 | Triangular prism (6 vertices) |
  | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Pyramid" | 3 | Square-base pyramid (5 vertices) |

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2); // Compute face-to-cell connectivity
  @endcode

  @see @ref guides-meshes "Meshes guide"
  @see @ref guides-polytopes "Polytopes guide"
  @see @ref guides-connectivity "Connectivity guide"
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Assembly
  @brief Global assembly of discrete operators.

  @ref Rodin::Assembly::Sequential "Assembly::Sequential" evaluates local integrals and
  accumulates global matrices and vectors; @ref Rodin::Assembly::Default "Assembly::Default"
  selects the configured assembly strategy. @ref
  Rodin::Assembly::BilinearFormAssemblyInput "BilinearFormAssemblyInput" supplies
  spaces and integrators, and @ref Rodin::Assembly::ConstraintMap "ConstraintMap"
  expands degree-of-freedom constraints when assembling constrained or coupled
  problems.

  # Assembly strategies

  | Strategy | Class | Description |
  |----------|-------|-------------|
  | **Sequential** | @ref Rodin::Assembly::Sequential "Assembly::Sequential" | Single-threaded assembly. Iterates over mesh cells, evaluates local element matrices/vectors, and accumulates into the global system. Deterministic and reproducible. |
  | **OpenMP** | @ref Rodin::Assembly "Assembly::OpenMP" | Shared-memory parallel assembly using OpenMP. Each thread processes a subset of cells and accumulates contributions using thread-safe operations. Requires compilation with `RODIN_USE_OPENMP=ON`. |
  | **Default** | @ref Rodin::Assembly::Default "Assembly::Default" | Compile-time policy selector. Maps to @ref Rodin::Assembly "Assembly::OpenMP" when OpenMP is available, otherwise falls back to @ref Rodin::Assembly::Sequential "Assembly::Sequential". Users rarely need to specify this directly. |

  # What gets assembled

  - **Bilinear Form** @f$ a(u,v) @f$: Assembles into a sparse matrix
    @f$ A_{ij} = a(\phi_j, \psi_i) @f$ where @f$ \phi_j @f$ are trial basis
    functions and @f$ \psi_i @f$ are test basis functions.
  - **Linear Form** @f$ \ell(v) @f$: Assembles into a vector
    @f$ b_i = \ell(\psi_i) @f$.
  - **Dirichlet BC**: Assembles into an `IndexMap` mapping constrained DOFs
    to their prescribed values.
  - **Problem**: Orchestrates the full assembly pipeline — bilinear form,
    linear form(s), Dirichlet elimination — into a complete
    @ref Rodin::Math::LinearSystem "LinearSystem<Operator, Vector>".

  # Assembly pipeline

  Calling a solver on a `Problem` normally triggers assembly. Explicit
  `Problem::assemble()` updates the system without solving it. The pipeline is:

  1. Assemble the bilinear form into a sparse matrix @f$ A @f$
  2. Assemble the linear form into a vector @f$ b @f$
  3. Apply Dirichlet boundary conditions (row/column elimination)
  4. Package into a `LinearSystem` and pass to the solver

  Users can also trigger assembly manually by calling `BilinearForm::assemble()`
  or `LinearForm::assemble()`.

  # Multiple-field assembly

  For saddle-point problems (e.g.\ Stokes), the Assembly module supports
  block-structured systems via `Tuple<BilinearForm...>` and
  `Tuple<LinearForm...>`, which assemble into block matrices and vectors.
  This is used by multi-field `Problem` objects with multiple trial/test
  function pairs.

  @see @ref guides-variational "Variational formulations guide"

  # Representative formula

  Trial basis functions @f$\phi_j@f$ and test basis functions @f$\psi_i@f$
  give the assembled matrix and load vector.
  @f[
    A_{ij}=a(\phi_j,\psi_i), \qquad b_i=\ell(\psi_i).
  @f]

 */


/**
  @namespace Rodin::Geometry::Euclidean
  @brief Module for dealing with Euclidean geometries.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::IO
  @brief Reading and writing meshes, fields, and associated data.

  @ref Rodin::IO::MeshLoader "MeshLoader" and @ref Rodin::IO::MeshPrinter
  "MeshPrinter" import and export meshes; @ref Rodin::IO::GridFunctionLoader
  "GridFunctionLoader" and @ref Rodin::IO::GridFunctionPrinter "GridFunctionPrinter"
  transfer discrete fields. Format-specific backends support MFEM, MEDIT, and HDF5
  data, while @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "IO::XDMF" writes meshes, multiple fields, and time
  series for visualization.

  Supported mesh and field formats include:

  - **MFEM** (@ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "IO::FileFormat::MFEM") — Native MFEM mesh and grid function format
  - **MEDIT** (@ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "IO::FileFormat::MEDIT") — MEDIT format used by MMG and INRIA tools
  - **HDF5** (@ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "IO::FileFormat::HDF5") — High-performance binary format

  Additionally, the @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "IO::XDMF" class provides XDMF 3.0
  output for scientific visualization in [ParaView](https://www.paraview.org/),
  supporting time series and multiple fields.

  # XDMF architecture

  The XDMF writer uses a two-level design:

  - @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "IO::XDMF" — Top-level writer that manages the `.xdmf` XML file and `.h5`
    HDF5 data. Provides `write()`, `flush()`, `close()`, `setPadding()`.
  - @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF::Grid "XDMF::Grid" — A lightweight handle returned by `xdmf.grid()` (default)
    or `xdmf.grid("name")` (named). Provides `setMesh()`, `add()`, `hasMesh()`,
    `reset()` — all returning `Grid&` for method chaining.

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("output");
  xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("u", u.getSolution());
  xdmf.write();
  @endcode

  For MPI-distributed I/O, pass the communicator.

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("distributed_output", MPI_COMM_WORLD);
  @endcode

  # File format summary

  | Format | Extensions | Key Classes | Notes |
  |--------|-----------|-------------|-------|
  | MFEM | `.mesh`, `.gf` | @ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "IO::FileFormat::MFEM" | Text-based, compatible with MFEM ecosystem |
  | MEDIT | `.mesh` | @ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "IO::FileFormat::MEDIT" | Used by MMG, supports attributes |
  | HDF5 | `.h5` | @ref Rodin::IO::FileFormat "IO::FileFormat::HDF5" | Binary, parallel-capable |
  | XDMF | `.xdmf` + `.h5` | @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "IO::XDMF", @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF::Grid "IO::XDMF::Grid" | ParaView visualization, time series |

  @see @ref guides-io "I/O in Rodin" for a comprehensive guide.
 */


/**
  @namespace Rodin::Variational
  @brief Finite element spaces, fields, and variational problems.

  @ref Rodin::Variational::H1 "H1" and @ref Rodin::Variational::P1 "P1" define
  finite element spaces, while @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction "GridFunction"
  evaluates and interpolates discrete fields. @ref Rodin::Variational::TrialFunction
  "TrialFunction", @ref Rodin::Variational::TestFunction "TestFunction", and @ref
  Rodin::Variational::Problem "Problem" express weak forms, boundary conditions, and
  the algebraic problems used to solve PDEs.

  # Finite element spaces

  - `P0` — Piecewise constant (discontinuous)
    finite element space
  - `P0g` — Global constant space (1 DOF for scalar, vdim for vector)
  - `P1` — Piecewise linear (continuous)
    finite element space
  - `H1` — Higher-order @f$ H^1 @f$-conforming
    finite element space (arbitrary polynomial order)

  # Functions

  - `TrialFunction` — Represents the
    unknown solution @f$ u @f$ in a variational formulation
  - `TestFunction` — Represents the
    weighting function @f$ v @f$
  - `GridFunction` — A concrete function
    storing DOF values in a finite element space

  # Variational forms

  The module supports building variational problems with the following weak form.
  @f[
    \text{Find} \quad u \in U \quad \text{ s.t. } \quad \forall v \in V \quad
    a(u, v) = b(v) \ ,
  @f]
  which are translated into linear algebra problems @f$ \mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}
  = \mathbf{b} @f$.

  Key expression classes:
  - `Integral(A, B)` — Domain integrals (bilinear or linear forms)
  - `BoundaryIntegral(A, B)` — Boundary integrals
  - `FaceIntegral(A, B)` — Integrals over all faces (interior + boundary)
  - `InterfaceIntegral(A, B)` — Integrals over interior faces only

  Differential operators:
  - `Grad(u)` — Gradient operator
  - `Div(u)` — Divergence operator
  - `Jacobian(u)` — Jacobian (gradient of vector field)
  - `Transpose(A)` — Matrix transpose

  Algebraic operators:
  - `Dot(A, B)` — Inner product / Frobenius contraction
  - `Trace(A)` — Matrix trace
  - `Frobenius(A)` — Frobenius norm
  - `Component(u, i)` — Extract @f$ i @f$-th component of vector function

  DG operators:
  - `Jump(u)` — Jump across internal faces: @f$ [u] = u^+ - u^- @f$
  - `Average(u)` — Average across internal faces: @f$ \{u\} = (u^+ + u^-)/2 @f$

  Boundary conditions:
  - `DirichletBC(u, g)` — Essential boundary conditions
  - `PeriodicBC(u, dofMap)` — Periodic boundary conditions

  Convenience functions:
  - `RealFunction` — Scalar function from constant or lambda
  - `VectorFunction` — Vector function from initializer list
  - `Zero` — Zero scalar/vector function
  - `Pow`, `Exp`, `Sqrt`, `Sin`, `Cos`, `Tan`, `Abs`, `Min`, `Max` — Pointwise math operations

  Special operators:
  - `BoundaryNormal(mesh)` — Outward unit normal on @f$ \partial\Omega @f$
  - `FaceNormal(mesh)` — Unit normal on mesh faces
  - `IdentityMatrix(n)` — @f$ n \times n @f$ identity matrix function
  - `F::x`, `F::y`, `F::z` — Coordinate projection functions
  - `Re(u)`, `Im(u)`, `Conjugate(u)` — Complex-valued function operators

  Advanced operators:
  - `Potential(K, u)` — Integral convolution: @f$ \int K(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}) u(\mathbf{y}) \, d\mathbf{y} @f$
  - `Flow(t, u, vel)` — Flow composition @f$ u \circ \Phi_t @f$ (characteristic tracing for advection)
  - `TraceOperator(u, attr)` — Boundary trace @f$ u|_{\partial\Omega} @f$
  - `LinearElasticityIntegral(u, v)(lambda, mu)` — Hooke elasticity bilinear form

  # Problem

  The `Problem` class assembles all forms
  and boundary conditions into a solvable linear system.

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);

  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
          - Integral(f, v)
          + DirichletBC(u, Zero());

  Solver::CG(poisson).solve();
  @endcode

  @see @ref guides-variational "Variational formulations guide"
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Distance
  @brief Distance propagation and Poisson-based level-set approximations.

  The unsigned distance to a geometric set @f$\Gamma@f$ is
  @f[
    d(x,\Gamma)=\inf_{y\in\Gamma}\lVert x-y\rVert.
  @f]
  Rodin provides fast-marching propagation, auxiliary Poisson fields and
  algebraic normalization. Their outputs and boundary data have distinct
  meanings; select and check the construction required by the application.

  # Propagation from an attributed interface

  @ref Rodin::Distance::Eikonal wraps unit-speed fast marching. Facets carrying
  the configured interface attributes supply zero-time seed vertices.
  `solve()` propagates nonnegative arrival values. `sign()` subsequently
  negates values at vertices belonging to cells with the interior attributes.
  The interface should separate those regions consistently.

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 space(mesh);
  GridFunction distance(space);
  Distance::Eikonal eikonal(distance);
  eikonal.setInterface(interfaceAttr)
         .setInterior(interiorAttr)
         .solve()
         .sign();
  @endcode

  Prepare vertex adjacency and vertex/cell incidence before solving. The
  underlying local FMM uses scalar P1 storage. Its discrete arrival times
  approximate distance; geometry and stencil error require assessment.
  @ref examples-eikonal-fmm supplies an analytic point-source comparison.

  # Auxiliary Poisson fields

  @ref Rodin::Distance::Poisson solves @f$-\Delta u=1@f$ with zero data on
  the exterior boundary. Its scalar output is a Poisson potential. It is often
  used as input to a distance normalization.

  @ref Rodin::Distance::SignedPoisson has two call forms. The full-domain
  overload solves @f$-\Delta u=-1@f$ with zero exterior data. The attributed
  overload uses an interior cell region @f$\Omega_-@f$ and an interface
  @f$\Gamma@f$.
  @f[
    -\Delta u=1-2\chi_{\Omega_-},\qquad u|_\Gamma=0.
  @f]
  Thus the forcing is negative inside and positive outside. Remaining exterior
  boundary portions have the natural condition of the weak problem. These
  classes take a finite element space when invoked.

  @code{.cpp}
  auto positivePotential = Distance::Poisson()(space);
  auto negativePotential = Distance::SignedPoisson()(space);
  auto signedPotential = Distance::SignedPoisson()(interfaceAttr, interiorAttr, space);
  @endcode

  The attributed overload also accepts sets of attributes. The mesh must
  resolve and label the interface before the solve. The convenience operators
  use CG internally; application-level convergence diagnostics may require
  explicitly constructing the corresponding variational problem.

  # Algebraic normalization

  @ref Rodin::Distance::Rvachev and @ref Rodin::Distance::SpaldingTucker
  evaluate, respectively,
  @f[
    d_R=\frac{u}{\sqrt{u^2+\lVert\nabla u\rVert^2}},\qquad
    d_{ST}=\frac{2u}{\lVert\nabla u\rVert+
      \sqrt{\lVert\nabla u\rVert^2+2|u|}}.
  @f]
  The operators interpolate these expressions into a grid function on the
  input space. A nonzero denominator preserves the sign pointwise before
  interpolation. Both expressions are undefined where the field and gradient
  vanish together. An approximate distance interpretation requires suitable
  scaling of the input and comparison against the geometry of interest.
  Neither formula by itself establishes unit gradient throughout the domain.

  @code{.cpp}
  auto normalized = Distance::SpaldingTucker()(signedPotential);
  @endcode

  Gradients of piecewise fields need a consistent one-sided interpretation at
  interfaces. See @ref guides-interfaces-dg for traces, and
  @ref guides-shape-optimization for the role of redistancing in transport.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Hilbert
  @brief Hilbert-space formulations and regularization.

  @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "getTrialFunction() / getTestFunction()" solves a regularized extension problem that
  converts a linear functional into a smooth field over the mesh. It is commonly
  used to extend boundary shape derivatives into volume descent directions, with a
  configurable length scale controlling smoothing and boundary conditions
  constraining the extension.
  @f[
    \alpha^2 \int_\Omega \nabla u : \nabla v \, dx + \int_\Omega u \cdot v \, dx = \ell(v)
  @f]
  where @f$ \alpha @f$ is a regularization length-scale parameter that controls
  the smoothness of the extended field (larger @f$ \alpha @f$ = smoother).

  This is used, for example, to extend and regularize a shape derivative
  (defined only on the boundary) to a smooth descent direction in the full
  computational domain.

  # API

  | Method | Description |
  |--------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "H1a(fes)" | Creates the extension operator on a finite element space |
  | @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "setAlpha(alpha)" | Sets @f$ \alpha @f$ (typically proportional to mesh size @f$ h @f$) |
  | @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "operator()(lf)" | Applies extension to a linear form (differential) |
  | @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "operator+=(DirichletBC)" | Adds boundary conditions to the extension problem |
  | @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "getProblem()" | Access the underlying @ref Rodin::Variational::Problem "Problem" object |
  | @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "getTrialFunction() / getTestFunction()" | Access trial/test functions |

  For vector-valued problems, @ref Rodin::Hilbert::H1a "H1a" uses `Jacobian()` instead of `Grad()`.
  The internal solver is `Solver::CG`.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Advection
  @brief Transport and advection of fields.

  @ref Rodin::Advection::Lagrangian "Lagrangian" advances scalar fields on
  unstructured meshes by tracing characteristics and projecting the transported
  field into a finite element space. It is used to transport level sets and other
  evolving fields; boundary policies control the treatment of characteristics that
  reach the mesh boundary.
  @f[
    \frac{\partial \phi}{\partial t} + \mathbf{v} \cdot \nabla \phi = 0
  @f]

  # Algorithm

  At each time step, the Lagrangian method:
  1. **Traces characteristics** backward from each mesh vertex along the
     velocity field @f$ \mathbf{v} @f$ for a duration @f$ \Delta t @f$
     using the `Flow` operator
  2. **Interpolates** the solution at the departure points
  3. **Projects** the interpolated values back onto the finite element space
     via an @f$ L^2 @f$ projection (mass-matrix solve using CG)

  Each step solves the following variational problem.
  @f[
    \int_\Omega u^{n+1} v \, dx = \int_\Omega u^n(\mathbf{X}) \, v \, dx
  @f]
  where @f$ \mathbf{X} @f$ is the departure point traced backward along
  @f$ \mathbf{v} @f$.

  # Time stepping and boundary policies

  The characteristic tracing uses configurable time-stepping schemes. The
  default is Runge-Kutta 4 (`Math::RungeKutta::RK4`); custom steppers can
  be passed as the fifth constructor argument.

  Two boundary policies handle characteristics that hit the domain boundary:

  | Policy | Behavior |
  |--------|----------|
  | @ref Rodin::Advection::StopInsideBoundaryPolicy "StopInsideBoundaryPolicy" | Clamps the departure point to an interior point with a small inward offset (`eps_in_phys`, default @f$ 10^{-12} @f$). Used automatically for the **first time step** (from initial condition). |
  | @ref Rodin::Advection::TaylorBoundaryShiftPolicy "TaylorBoundaryShiftPolicy<Field>" | Applies a first-order Taylor correction at the boundary hit by shifting the point inward and accumulating the gradient correction @f$ \nabla\phi \cdot \delta\mathbf{x} @f$. Used automatically for **subsequent steps** (from the current solution). |

  The `Lagrangian` class tracks time internally (`m_t`) and switches between
  the initial condition (first step) and the current solution (later steps).

  # Typical usage

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);

  GridFunction phi(Vh);
  // ... initialize phi ...

  VectorFunction velocity{1.0, 0.0};  // uniform horizontal advection

  // Default stepper: RK4
  Advection::Lagrangian advection(u, v, phi, velocity);

  Real dt = 0.01;
  for (int step = 0; step < 100; ++step)
    advection.step(dt);
  @endcode

  @note The module is header-only (`Rodin::Advection` INTERFACE library).

  @see `Flow` for the characteristic tracer.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Eikonal
  @brief Eikonal equations and distance propagation.

  @ref Rodin::Eikonal::FMM "FMM" implements fast marching on simplicial meshes,
  propagating arrival times from prescribed seed vertices. It provides the
  underlying distance propagation used by level-set reinitialization and
  distance-field construction, with a priority queue controlling the order of vertex
  updates.
  @f[
    F(x)\lVert\nabla u\rVert = 1
  @f] where @f$F(x)>0@f$ is propagation speed. When @f$ F = 1 @f$, the solution represents the distance function.

  # Fast marching method (FMM)

  The primary solver is @ref Rodin::Eikonal::FMM "FMM", which implements the
  Fast Marching Method on unstructured simplicial meshes. The algorithm
  propagates a wavefront from seed vertices, updating arrival times in
  causal order using a priority queue. Vertex states during propagation:

  | State | Description |
  |-------|-------------|
  | **Far** | Not yet visited |
  | **Considered** | In the priority queue, tentative value computed |
  | **Accepted** | Finalized by the discrete propagation scheme |

  The FMM specialization for P1 finite elements on local meshes supports:
  - Local triangular and tetrahedral meshes, including embedded triangular surfaces
  - User-defined speed functions @f$ F(x) @f$
  - Seed interface specification (initial zero-distance vertices)

  # Typical usage

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  GridFunction u(Vh);

  // Speed function F(x) = 1 (distance computation)
  auto speed = [](const Geometry::Point&) -> Real { return 1.0; };

  Eikonal::FMM fmm(u, speed);
  fmm.seed(interfaceVertices).solve();
  @endcode

  See @ref examples-eikonal-fmm for a complete point-source example with
  analytic errors and a speed-scaling check.

  @note The module is header-only.

  @see @ref Rodin::Distance "Distance module" for higher-level distance
  function computation that wraps FMM.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Solid
  @brief Finite-strain kinematics, constitutive responses and virtual-work forms.

  A total-Lagrangian hyperelastic model uses a reference domain
  @f$\Omega_0@f$, displacement @f$u@f$ and deformation gradient
  @f$F=I+\nabla_0u@f$. The stored energy determines the first Piola–Kirchhoff
  stress and tangent action.
  @f[
    P(F)=\frac{\partial W}{\partial F},\qquad
    DP(F)[H]=\frac{\partial^2 W}{\partial F^2}:H.
  @f]
  The @ref guides-solid-mechanics "solid mechanics guide" derives the weak
  equations and explains the reference/current configurations.

  # Kinematics and constitutive input

  @ref Rodin::Solid::KinematicState accepts a displacement gradient and
  exposes the deformation measures used by a law:

  | Getter | Quantity |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | `getDeformationGradient()` | @f$F=I+\nabla_0u@f$ |
  | `getDeformationGradientInverse()` | @f$F^{-1}@f$ |
  | `getDeformationGradientInverseTranspose()` | @f$F^{-T}@f$ |
  | `getRightCauchyGreenTensor()` | @f$C=F^TF@f$ |
  | `getLeftCauchyGreenTensor()` | @f$b=FF^T@f$ |
  | `getJacobian()` | @f$J=\det F@f$ |
  | `getLogJacobian()` | @f$\log J@f$ |

  Constitutive models involving @f$\log J@f$ and inverse deformation
  gradients require an admissible deformation, including @f$J>0@f$.
  @ref Rodin::Solid::ConstitutivePoint combines the kinematics, optional
  spatial location and tagged auxiliary inputs such as reference fibers and
  activation. Integrator input callbacks populate those values at quadrature
  points.

  # Prepare and evaluate a law

  @ref Rodin::Solid::HyperElasticLaw defines the common constitutive interface.
  Bind a constitutive point through `setConstitutivePoint(point)`, then read
  `getStrainEnergyDensity()`, `getFirstPiolaKirchhoffStress(P)` and
  `getMaterialTangent(dP,dF)`. Binding captures the inputs needed by the law.
  Each assembly worker uses its own prepared law; concurrent rebinding of
  the same object requires synchronization.

  For example, @ref Rodin::Solid::NeoHookean implements
  @f[
    W(F)=\frac{\mu}{2}(\operatorname{tr}(F^TF)-d)
      -\mu\log J+\frac{\lambda}{2}(\log J)^2.
  @f]
  The checked constitutive-response example varies
  @f$F=\operatorname{diag}(s,1,1)@f$ and compares a centered energy derivative
  with @f$P_{11}@f$ over 71 stretches. Its largest scaled discrepancy was
  @f$1.92\times10^{-10}@f$ with finite-difference increment @f$10^{-6}@f$.

  @snippet Solid/ConstitutiveResponse.cpp constitutive-bind

  Other laws include @ref Rodin::Solid::SaintVenantKirchhoff,
  @ref Rodin::Solid::MooneyRivlin and @ref Rodin::Solid::HolzapfelOgden.
  Their class pages specify energy, dimensions, parameters and auxiliary inputs.
  Choose units consistently for the geometry, material parameters and loads.

  # Assemble internal virtual work

  @ref Rodin::Solid::InternalVirtualWork binds a material law to the current
  displacement. Its residual and tangent represent
  @f[
    R(u;v)=\int_{\Omega_0}P(F):\nabla_0v\,dX,\qquad
    K(u;\delta u,v)=\int_{\Omega_0}
      DP(F)[\nabla_0\delta u]:\nabla_0v\,dX.
  @f]
  The façade supplies `Residual(v)`, `Tangent(du,v)` and their combined form.

  @code{.cpp}
  auto internal = Solid::InternalVirtualWork(law, displacement);
  Problem newton(du, v);
  newton = internal(du, v) - Integral(bodyForce, v)
         + DirichletBC(du, Zero()).on(fixedBoundary);
  @endcode

  Here `displacement` is the current state, `du` is its trial increment and
  `v` is the matching test function. Problem assembly negates the physical
  residual when placing linear forms on the right-hand side, giving
  @f$K\delta u=\ell-R@f$. Keep the state and forms valid throughout Newton
  updates. Nonpolynomial laws and curved geometry require quadrature assessed
  against the intended accuracy.

  # Postprocess stresses and strain

  @ref Rodin::Solid::FirstPiolaKirchhoffStress,
  @ref Rodin::Solid::CauchyStress and @ref Rodin::Solid::GreenLagrangeStrain
  expose constitutive fields. In particular,
  @f$\sigma=J^{-1}PF^T@f$ and @f$E=(F^TF-I)/2@f$.
  Interpolate or project these fields into an output space appropriate for
  their regularity, then export with @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF.

  @see @ref guides-solid-mechanics, @ref guides-solvers,
  @ref Rodin::Variational::LinearElasticityIntegral
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::QF
  @brief Reference integration formulas selected by geometry and exactness.

  A reference rule consists of sites @f$\widehat x_q@f$ and weights @f$w_q@f$.
  @f[
    \int_{\widehat K}f(\widehat x)\,d\widehat x
      \approx\sum_qw_qf(\widehat x_q).
  @f]
  @ref Rodin::QF::QuadratureFormula::get selects a persistent formula for a
  requested polynomial exactness and reference geometry. The
  @ref guides-quadrature "quadrature guide" explains mapping to physical
  cells, ownership, exactness ranges and refinement checks.

  # Select a formula

  @code{.cpp}
  const auto& formula = QF::QuadratureFormula::get(4, Polytope::Type::Triangle);
  const auto& quadrature = cell.getQuadrature(formula);
  @endcode

  The request is an exactness degree. Concrete family constructors may use a
  different parameter. For example, `GaussLegendre(Segment,n)` takes a point
  count and integrates univariate polynomials through degree @f$2n-1@f$.

  # Formula families

  | Family | Role |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref Rodin::QF::XiaoGimbutas | Native tabulated simplex rules within their available ranges |
  | @ref Rodin::QF::WitherdenVincent | Native tabulated pyramid rules within their available range |
  | @ref Rodin::QF::GaussLegendre | Segment rules and positive collapsed-coordinate constructions |
  | @ref Rodin::QF::TensorProduct | Products for quadrilaterals, hexahedra and wedges |
  | @ref Rodin::QF::GaussLobatto | Endpoint-inclusive segment rules |
  | @ref Rodin::QF::GrundmannMoller | A separately selectable simplex family |
  | @ref Rodin::QF::Centroid | A one-site reference rule, also used for point geometry |

  Default selection uses native tables when available and the configured
  positive conical-product fallback beyond them. Product cells combine the
  corresponding factor rules. Pyramid collapse contributes its Jacobian to
  the Gauss–Jacobi weight. Point, segment, triangle, quadrilateral,
  tetrahedron, hexahedron, wedge and pyramid geometries are supported.

  Polynomial exactness applies to the complete reference integrand. Curved
  mappings, rational bases and nonpolynomial coefficients require attention
  to the mapped integrand and numerical refinement. The checked
  `Geometry/Quadrature.cpp` example in @ref guides-quadrature integrates both
  a reference polynomial and its mapped physical counterpart.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::FormLanguage
  @brief Typed expressions for constructing variational forms.

  An expression's C++ type records its operands and algebraic role. Function
  expressions represent known fields; shape-function expressions carry a
  trial or test slot; bilinear integrators pair those slots. Traits propagate
  scalar type, range and finite element space through composition.

  @code{.cpp}
  auto stiffness = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v));
  auto load = Integral(f, v);
  Problem problem(u, v);
  problem = stiffness - load + DirichletBC(u, Zero());
  @endcode

  # Identity, copying and ownership

  @ref Rodin::FormLanguage::Base supplies identity and polymorphic copying.
  Expression nodes generally own copies of their child expressions, created
  with `copy()`. @ref Rodin::FormLanguage::List stores polymorphic elements
  with value semantics through owned pointers. Each owned child is
  released with its owning expression.

  Copying an expression does not necessarily copy its underlying mesh or
  solution coefficients. A grid-function expression can still refer to its
  finite element space and field storage. Keep those objects alive while the
  assembled forms use them; rebuilding a mesh or space requires reviewing
  dependent expressions and systems.

  # Evaluation and assembly

  @ref Rodin::FormLanguage::Traits describes the associated types of each
  node family. CRTP dispatch evaluates known-field expressions at geometric
  points; assembly supplies basis functions and quadrature to realize linear
  and bilinear forms. Constructing a form records that composition. A solver
  assembles and evaluates it according to the selected backend.

  Evaluation and assembly may use mutable working state. Follow the
  @ref guides-thread-safety "thread-safety guide" when sharing expressions
  across workers.

  @see @ref guides-variational, @ref guides-numerical-contracts
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Math
  @brief Spatial arithmetic, matrix operations, and sparse linear algebra.

  @ref Rodin::Math::SpatialVector "SpatialVector<T>" and @ref
  Rodin::Math::SpatialMatrix "SpatialMatrix<T>" support small spatial calculations
  without heap allocation. @ref Rodin::Math::Vector "Vector<T>", @ref
  Rodin::Math::Matrix "Matrix<T>", and their fixed-size counterparts provide
  Eigen-compatible algebra; @ref Rodin::Math::LinearSystem "LinearSystem<Operator, Vector>" groups
  system data and constraint elimination. These types support geometric mappings,
  constitutive calculations, assembly, and linear solves.

  # Types

  | Symbol | Type | Description |
  |--------|------|-------------|
  | @f$ x \in \mathbb{K}^{n},\ n \in \mathbb{N} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::Vector "Vector<T>" | Dynamic dense column vector (Eigen-based) |
  | @f$ A \in \mathbb{K}^{m \times n},\ m,n \in \mathbb{N} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::Matrix "Matrix<T>" | Dynamic dense matrix (Eigen-based) |
  | @f$ \begin{gathered} A_{\mathrm{CSC}} \in \mathbb{K}^{m \times n} \\ (A_{\mathrm{CSC}})_{r_k,j}=v_k,\ p_j \leq k < p_{j+1} \end{gathered} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::SparseMatrix "SparseMatrix<T>" | CSC sparse matrix with values @f$ v_k @f$;<br>row indices @f$ r_k @f$;<br>column offsets @f$ p_j @f$. |
  | @f$ x \in \mathbb{K}^{n},\ n \in \mathbb{N},\ n\text{ fixed} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::FixedSizeVector "FixedSizeVector<T, Size>" | Dense vector with its dimension fixed at compile time |
  | @f$ A \in \mathbb{K}^{m \times n},\ m,n \in \mathbb{N},\ m,n\text{ fixed} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::FixedSizeMatrix "FixedSizeMatrix<T, Rows, Cols>" | Dense matrix with its dimensions fixed at compile time |
  | @f$ x \in \mathbb{K}^{n},\ n \leq 3 @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::SpatialVector "SpatialVector<T>" | Small vector with runtime dimension for physical coordinates |
  | @f$ A \in \mathbb{K}^{m \times n},\ m,n \leq 3 @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::SpatialMatrix "SpatialMatrix<T>" | Small matrix with runtime dimensions for Jacobians and stress tensors |
  | @f$ A x = b @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::LinearSystem "LinearSystem<Operator, Vector>" | Groups an operator, solution vector, and right-hand side for a linear solve |

  Here, @f$ \mathbb{K} @f$ is either the real field @f$ \mathbb{R} @f$ or the
  complex field @f$ \mathbb{C} @f$.

  # Constants

  | Symbol | Function | Description |
  |--------|----------|-------------|
  | @f$ \pi @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::Constants::pi() "Constants::pi()" | Ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter. |

  # Mathematical functions

  | Symbol | Function | Domain | Description |
  |--------|----------|--------|-------------|
  | @f$ \lvert x \rvert @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "abs(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Absolute value or magnitude |
  | @f$ e^x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "exp(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Exponential function |
  | @f$ \sqrt{x} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "sqrt(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Square root |
  | @f$ x^y @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "pow(x, y)" | Scalar or matrix | Power function |
  | @f$ x^2 @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "pow2(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Square of a value |
  | @f$ \ln x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "log(x)" | Scalar | Natural logarithm |
  | @f$ \log_2 x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "log2(x)" | Scalar | Base-2 logarithm |
  | @f$ \log_{10} x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "log10(x)" | Scalar | Base-10 logarithm |
  | @f$ \sin x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "sin(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Sine |
  | @f$ \cos x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "cos(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Cosine |
  | @f$ \tan x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "tan(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Tangent |
  | @f$ \arcsin x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "asin(x)" | Scalar, @f$ x \in [-1, 1] @f$ | Inverse sine in radians |
  | @f$ \arccos x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "acos(x)" | Scalar, @f$ x \in [-1, 1] @f$ | Inverse cosine in radians |
  | @f$ \arctan x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "atan(x)" | Scalar | Inverse tangent in radians |
  | @f$ \operatorname{atan2}(y, x) @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "atan2(y, x)" | Two real scalar arguments | Two-argument inverse tangent in radians |
  | @f$ \sinh x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "sinh(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Hyperbolic sine |
  | @f$ \cosh x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "cosh(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Hyperbolic cosine |
  | @f$ \tanh x @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "tanh(x)" | Scalar or matrix | Hyperbolic tangent |
  | @f$ \min(a, b) @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "min(a, b)" | Comparable values | Smaller of two values |
  | @f$ \max(a, b) @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "max(a, b)" | Comparable values | Larger of two values |
  | @f$ \operatorname{clamp}(x, lo, hi) @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math "clamp(x, lo, hi)" | Comparable values | Restricts a value to the given bounds |

  # Unit types

  | Symbol | Type | Description |
  |--------|------|-------------|
  | @f$ \theta\,\mathrm{rad} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::Rad "Rad" | Radian wrapper with explicit conversion to `Real` |
  | @f$ \theta^{\circ} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::Deg "Deg" | Degree wrapper with conversion to `Rad` |

  # Runge–Kutta schemes

  | Symbol | Method | Order | Description |
  |--------|--------|-------|-------------|
  | @f$ q_{n+1}=q_n+\Delta t\,k_2 @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::RungeKutta::RK2 "RungeKutta::RK2" | 2 | Midpoint method |
  | @f$ q_{n+1}=q_n+\frac{\Delta t}{6}(k_1+2k_2+2k_3+k_4) @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::RungeKutta::RK4 "RungeKutta::RK4" | 4 | Classical Runge-Kutta method |

  # Root finding

  | Symbol | Method | Description |
  |--------|--------|-------------|
  | @f$ x_{n+1}=x_n-\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::RootFinding::NewtonRaphson "RootFinding::NewtonRaphson<Scalar>" | Finds scalar roots with a safeguarded Newton iteration, interval bracketing, and bisection fallback |

  # Linear algebra utilities

  | Operation | Function | Description |
  |-----------|----------|-------------|
  | @f$ a \cdot b @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::dot "dot(a, b)" | Inner product; conjugates the right operand for complex values |
  | @f$ a \times b @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::SpatialVector::cross "a.cross(b)" | Three-dimensional cross product |
  | @f$ a + b @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::sum "sum(a, b)" | Adds two values |
  | @f$ a - b @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::minus "minus(a, b)" | Subtracts two values; @ref Rodin::Math::minus "minus(a)" negates one value |
  | @f$ a b @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::mult "mult(a, b)" | Multiplies two values |
  | @f$ a / b @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::division "division(a, b)" | Divides one value by another |
  | @f$ \overline{a} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::conj "conj(a)" | Computes the complex conjugate |
  | @f$ \mathrm{NaN} @f$ | @ref Rodin::Math::nan "nan<T>()" | Returns a quiet NaN sentinel of type `T` |

  # Usage

  Include `<Rodin/Math.h>` and `<utility>` for these examples. The names below
  assume `using namespace Rodin;` and `using namespace Rodin::Math;`.

  ## Spatial arithmetic

  Small spatial vectors support scalar products, cross products, and norms.

  @code{.cpp}
  SpatialVector<Real> a{1, 2, 3};
  SpatialVector<Real> b{4, 5, 6};
  const Real product = dot(a, b);       // 32
  const auto normal = a.cross(b);      // {-3, 6, -3}
  const Real length = a.norm();        // sqrt(14)
  @endcode

  ## Dense and fixed-size algebra

  Dynamic and fixed-size types support Eigen-compatible initialization and
  expressions. Store an expression in a concrete type to retain its result.

  @code{.cpp}
  Matrix<Real> A(2, 2);
  A << 2, -1,
      -1,  2;
  Vector<Real> x(2);
  x << 1, 2;
  Vector<Real> y = A * x;              // {0, 3}

  FixedSizeMatrix<Real, 2, 2> fixedA = A;
  FixedSizeVector<Real, 2> fixedX = x;
  FixedSizeVector<Real, 2> fixedY = fixedA * fixedX;
  @endcode

  ## Sparse matrices

  Insert entries and compress the matrix before using its CSC storage.

  @code{.cpp}
  SparseMatrix<Real> A(3, 3);
  A.insert(0, 0) = 2;
  A.insert(1, 1) = 3;
  A.insert(2, 2) = 4;
  A.makeCompressed();

  Vector<Real> x = Vector<Real>::Ones(3);
  Vector<Real> y = A * x;              // {2, 3, 4}
  @endcode

  ## Complex scalar products

  The scalar product conjugates its right-hand operand.
  @f[
    a\cdot b=\sum_i a_i\overline{b_i}.
  @f]

  @code{.cpp}
  SpatialVector<Complex> a{Complex(1, 1), Complex(2, 0)};
  SpatialVector<Complex> b{Complex(0, 1), Complex(3, 0)};
  const Complex product = dot(a, b);   // 7 - i
  @endcode

  ## Angles and constants

  Convert degrees to radians explicitly; mathematical functions accept the
  underlying scalar value.

  @code{.cpp}
  const Rad angle = Deg(180).toRad();
  const Real radians = Real(angle);    // pi
  const Real halfTurn = Constants::pi();
  const Real sine = sin(radians);      // approximately zero
  @endcode

  ## Time integration

  Advance @f$ \dot{q}=-q @f$ by one time step with the classical fourth-order
  Runge-Kutta method. Supply the current state separately from the output.

  @code{.cpp}
  const Real current = 1;
  Real next = 0;
  RungeKutta::RK4 integrator;
  integrator.step(next, 0.1, current, [](Real q) { return -q; });
  // next approximates exp(-0.1).
  @endcode

  ## Scalar root finding

  Find @f$ \sqrt{2} @f$ by solving @f$ f(x)=x^2-2=0 @f$ in a bracket.
  The callable returns the function value and derivative; the result is an
  `Optional` containing the root when the iteration succeeds.

  @code{.cpp}
  RootFinding::NewtonRaphson<Real> solver;
  const auto root = solver.solve(
    [](Real x) { return std::make_pair(x * x - 2, 2 * x); },
    1.5, 1.0, 2.0);
  @endcode

 */


/**
  @namespace Rodin::Solver
  @brief Solution of linear and nonlinear discrete systems.

  @f[
    \text{Find } \mathbf{x} \text{ such that } A\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}.
  @f]

  @ref Rodin::Solver::CG "CG" handles symmetric positive-definite systems, @ref
  Rodin::Solver::GMRES "GMRES" handles general iterative solves, and @ref
  Rodin::Solver::SparseLU "SparseLU" provides direct sparse factorization. @ref
  Rodin::Solver::NewtonSolver "NewtonSolver" drives nonlinear solves. These solvers
  operate on prepared systems or variational problems and provide convergence
  controls for PDE and optimization calculations.

  Direct solvers (exact up to floating-point precision):
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SparseLU "SparseLU" — General-purpose sparse LU factorization
  - `UMFPack` — SuiteSparse UMFPACK solver
  - `CHOLMOD` — SuiteSparse Cholesky for SPD systems
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SimplicialLLT "SimplicialLLT" — Simplicial @f$ LL^T @f$
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SimplicialLDLT "SimplicialLDLT" — Simplicial @f$ LDL^T @f$
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::SparseQR "SparseQR" — Sparse QR factorization
  - `SPQR` — SuiteSparse sparse QR
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::HouseholderQR "HouseholderQR" — Dense Householder QR
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::LDLT "LDLT" — Dense @f$ LDL^T @f$

  Iterative solvers (for large systems):
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::CG "CG" — Conjugate Gradient (SPD systems)
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::BiCGSTAB "BiCGSTAB" — Bi-conjugate gradient stabilized
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::GMRES "GMRES" — Generalized Minimum Residual
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::DGMRES "DGMRES" — Deflated GMRES
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::MINRES "MINRES" — Minimum Residual (symmetric systems)
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::IDRS "IDRS" — Induced Dimension Reduction
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::IDRSTABL "IDRSTABL" — IDR(s) with stabilization
  - @ref Rodin::Solver::LeastSquaresCG "LeastSquaresCG" — Least-squares CG

  A solver can be constructed from an assembled form or problem.
  @code{.cpp}
  // Inline iterative solver — most common pattern
  Solver::CG(problem).solve();

  // Configured iterative solver
  Solver::CG solver(problem);
  solver.setMaxIterations(1000);
  solver.setTolerance(1e-10);
  solver.solve();

  // Direct solver
  Solver::SparseLU(problem).solve();
  @endcode

  Nonlinear solvers (for hyperelastic or other nonlinear problems):
  - `Solver::NewtonSolver<LinearSolver>` — Newton-Raphson iteration for nonlinear
    variational problems. At each iterate @f$ u^k @f$, assembles the tangent
    system @f$ J(u^k)\,\delta u^k = -F(u^k) @f$ and updates
    @f$ u^{k+1} = u^k + \delta u^k @f$. Configurable via `setMaxIterations()`,
    `setAbsoluteTolerance()`, and `setRelativeTolerance()`.

  @see @ref guides-solvers "Solvers guide"
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Test
  @brief Helpers for constructing numerical tests.

  @ref Rodin::Test::FDProbe "FDProbe" compares assembled tangents with finite
  differences and returns error diagnostics in @ref Rodin::Test::FDProbeReport
  "FDProbeReport". @ref Rodin::Test::Utility::ExpectedResultTable
  "ExpectedResultTable" organizes known input-output cases, and random generators
  supply test samples. These helpers support numerical regression tests for
  constitutive laws, geometry, and variational operators.

  For Rodin's convention
  @f$A(u)\Delta u=b(u)=-R(u)@f$, the consistency check is
  @f[
    A(u)w \approx -\frac{b(u+\epsilon w)-b(u-\epsilon w)}{2\epsilon}.
  @f]
  The probe reports absolute and relative errors. Table-driven helpers and
  random samplers support complementary numerical tests.

 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Utility
  @brief Type utilities and generic programming helpers.

  @ref Rodin::Utility::ParameterPack "ParameterPack" and @ref Rodin::Utility::Zip
  "Zip" support compile-time type and parameter manipulation. @ref
  Rodin::Utility::Overloaded "Overloaded" combines callable visitors, and @ref
  Rodin::Utility::OptionalReference "OptionalReference" represents optional
  non-owning access. These helpers support generic expressions, type dispatch, and
  reusable library extensions.

  For example,
  @ref Rodin::Utility::Overloaded "Overloaded" builds a visitor from lambdas.

  @code{.cpp}
  std::variant<int, Real> value = 2;
  const Real result = std::visit(Utility::Overloaded{
    [](int x) { return Real(x); },
    [](Real x) { return x; }
  }, value);
  @endcode

 */


/**
  @namespace Rodin::MMG
  @brief Mesh adaptation through the MMG integration.

  @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "MMG::Mesh" connects Rodin geometry to MMG. @ref
  Rodin::MMG::Adapt "MMG::Adapt" performs metric-driven adaptation, @ref
  Rodin::MMG::Optimizer "MMG::Optimizer" improves mesh quality, and @ref
  Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "LevelSetDiscretizer" fits or extracts an
  isosurface. These workflows support remeshing and interface reconstruction in
  shape optimization and evolving-geometry simulations.

  # Key classes

  | Class | Purpose |
  |-------|---------|
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "MMG::Mesh" | Local mesh type extending @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Geometry::Mesh<Context::Local>" with corner, ridge, and required-entity metadata. |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Adapt "MMG::Adapt" | Metric-based adaptation from a nodal size map. |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Optimizer "MMG::Optimizer" | Mesh-quality optimization without an external metric field. |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer" | Cuts a background mesh along an isovalue or extracts the isosurface. |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Base "MMG::Base" | Shared MMG remeshing configuration inherited by the workflow classes. |

  # Workflow semantics

  `Optimizer`, `Adapt`, and `LevelSetDiscretizer` are intentionally different
  operations:

  | Workflow | Expected behavior |
  |----------|-------------------|
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Optimizer "MMG::Optimizer" | Improves element quality while keeping the mesh close to the existing local size distribution. |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Adapt "MMG::Adapt" | Refines, coarsens, moves, and reconnects elements according to a size field. |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "noSplit(ref)" | Alters topology as needed to conform the output mesh to the selected level-set isovalue. |

  All workflows convert an @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "MMG::Mesh" to native MMG
  structures, run MMG, and convert the result back. The output is a remeshed
  object; input element indices should be treated as stable only where the
  wrapper or tests explicitly document preservation.

  # Backend selection

  The wrappers auto-dispatch to the appropriate MMG backend based on the mesh
  type:

  | Mesh type | Backend |
  |-----------|---------|
  | 2D planar | MMG2D |
  | 3D volumetric | MMG3D |
  | Surface in 3D | MMGS |

  # Shared configuration

  The MMG wrappers inherit common configuration methods from
  @ref Rodin::MMG::Base "setAngleDetection(bool)"; each returns `*this` for chaining:

  | Method | Description |
  |--------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Base "setHMin(hmin)" | Minimal allowed edge size |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Base "setHMax(hmax)" | Maximal allowed edge size |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Base "setHausdorff(hausd)" | Hausdorff distance tolerance for boundary approximation |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Base "setGradation(hgrad)" | Edge-size gradation ratio between adjacent elements |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Base "setAngleDetection(bool)" | Enable ridge angle detection for feature preservation |

  # Native MMG V5 API

  Native mesh handles are managed by @ref Rodin::MMG::V5::Mesh, with
  conversions named @ref Rodin::MMG::V5::Mesh::toV5 and
  @ref Rodin::MMG::V5::Mesh::fromV5. Native solution handles and grid-function
  transfers are provided by @ref Rodin::MMG::V5::Solution.

  | Namespace | Operations |
  |-----------|------------|
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::V5::Mesh | `create`, `copy`, `isSurface`, `destroy`, `toV5`, `fromV5` |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::V5::Solution | `create`, `copy`, `swap`, `destroy` |

  # Required entities

  @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "MMG::Mesh" stores MMG-specific metadata:

  | Method | Description |
  |--------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "setCorner(vertexIdx)" | Mark a vertex as a geometric corner |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "setRidge(edgeIdx)" | Mark an edge as a geometric ridge |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "setRequiredVertex(idx)" | Mark a vertex as required (`MG_REQ`) |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "setRequiredEdge(idx)" | Mark an edge as required (`MG_REQ`) |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "setRequiredTriangle(idx)" | Mark a triangle as required (`MG_REQ`) |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "setRequiredTetrahedron(idx)" | Mark a tetrahedron as required (`MG_REQ`) |

  Required vertices, edges, triangles, and tetrahedra are serialized through
  MEDIT, converted to native MMG `MG_REQ` tags, and restored from native MMG
  output. The optimizer and level-set wrappers restore required index sets when
  the original indices are still valid after remeshing.
  In MMG3D, `setRequiredTriangle(idx)` applies to boundary triangles exported
  to MMG's triangle table; interior faces of a tetrahedral mesh are not exported
  as MMG boundary triangles when face-cell incidence is available.

  For level-set discretization, the tested behavior is:

  - required entities not crossed by the level set retain their required
    metadata and original geometry in the 2D/3D cases covered by the test
    suite;
  - MMG may replace required entities crossed by the level set to conform the mesh to
  the isovalue, even when
    `MG_REQ`, individual labels, adjacent tetrahedron labels, local closure
    requirements, or `noSplit(ref)` are used.

  See @ref examples-mmg-behavior "MMG behavior and guarantees" for the full
  characterization.

  # LevelSetDiscretizer API

  In addition to @ref Rodin::MMG::Base "setAngleDetection(bool)" parameters,
  @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "noSplit(ref)" provides:

  | Method | Description |
  |--------|-------------|
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "setLevelSet(ls)" | Isovalue to extract (default 0.0) |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "surface(bool)" | When true, discretize the isosurface; when false, discretize the subdomain volume |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "setRMC(rmc)" | Removal threshold for small parasitic components |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "setBoundaryReference(ref)" | Material reference for the discretized boundary |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "setBaseReferences(refs)" | Restrict level-set splitting to specified base materials |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "split(ref, split)" | Split one material reference into inside/outside references |
  | @ref Rodin::MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer "noSplit(ref)" | Keep one material reference from being split by the material policy |

  `noSplit(ref)` controls material-reference splitting. MMG can modify intersected
  edges, triangles and tetrahedra to construct the level-set interface.

  @code{.cpp}
  MMG::Mesh cut = MMG::LevelSetDiscretizer()
    .setLevelSet(0.0)
    .split(interior, { interior, exterior })
    .setBoundaryReference(interface)
    .discretize(phi);
  @endcode

  Use @ref Rodin::MMG::Mesh "MMG::Mesh::Build()" to construct meshes
  programmatically via the builder pattern.

  The MMG dependency is configured by Rodin’s bundled dependency setup.
  See @ref examples-swift-background for adaptation followed by interface fitting.

  @see @ref examples-mmg-index "MMG examples"
  @see @ref examples-mmg-behavior "MMG behavior and guarantees"
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::Scotch
  @brief Graph partitioning through the Scotch integration.

  @code{.cpp}
  Scotch::Partitioner partitioner(mesh);
  partitioner.partition(numRanks);
  @endcode

  @ref Rodin::Scotch::Partitioner "Partitioner" builds a graph-based partition of
  mesh cells using Scotch. The resulting cell assignments feed mesh sharding and
  distribution, helping divide finite element workloads across MPI ranks while
  limiting connections between partitions.

  The primary class is `Scotch::Partitioner`, which partitions cells of
  a local mesh into a given number of parts. The resulting partition can be
  used with the `Sharder` to distribute the mesh for MPI computations.

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Scotch/MeshPartitioner.h>

  Scotch::Partitioner partitioner(mesh);
  partitioner.partition(numRanks);  // partition into numRanks parts

  size_t part = partitioner.getPartition(cellIndex);
  @endcode

  @note Requires the Scotch library and is conditionally compiled.
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::MPI
  @brief Distributed meshes, finite element spaces, and search operations.

  @code{.cpp}
  using DistributedMesh = Geometry::Mesh<Context::MPI>;
  @endcode

  @ref Rodin::Context::MPI "Context::MPI" supplies the distributed execution
  context, while @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh "Mesh<Context::MPI>" manages distributed
  mesh entities and connectivity. @ref Rodin::MPI::Sharder "Sharder" partitions mesh
  data for transfer between ranks. Distributed finite element spaces, point
  location, and assembly build on these components for multi-process PDE
  calculations.

  # Key components

  - @ref Rodin::Context::MPI "Context::MPI" — Wraps a `boost::mpi::environment`
    and `boost::mpi::communicator` to provide a uniform execution context.
  - @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh<Context::MPI> "Mesh\<Context::MPI\>" — Distributed
    mesh storing a rank-local @ref Rodin::Geometry::Shard "Shard" with ownership,
    ghost, and distributed index metadata.  Geometric queries
    (`getPolytopeCount`, `getVolume`, `getPerimeter`, etc.) perform
    MPI reductions automatically.
  - @ref Rodin::Geometry::Sharder<Context::MPI> "Sharder" — Distributes a global
    mesh across MPI ranks through `shard()`, `scatter()` and `gather()` in sequence.
    Also available as one-step `distribute()`.
  - @ref Rodin::Geometry::Shard "Shard" — Rank-local view of a partitioned mesh.
    Classifies every polytope as **Owned**, **Shared**, or **Ghost** and maintains
    bidirectional local↔distributed index maps.
  - `reconcile(d)` — After local connectivity discovery, reconciles entities of
    dimension @p d across ranks so that shared entities have consistent
    distributed IDs and ownership.

  # Typical workflow

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/MPI.h>

  boost::mpi::environment env(argc, argv);
  boost::mpi::communicator world;
  Context::MPI mpi(env, world);

  // Option A: UniformGrid (partition + distribute in one call)
  auto mesh = Mesh<Context::MPI>::UniformGrid(mpi, Polytope::Type::Tetrahedron, {16,16,16});

  // Option B: explicit partition → shard → scatter → gather
  MPI::Sharder sharder(mpi);
  if (world.rank() == 0)
  {
    LocalMesh mesh;
    mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Tetrahedron, {16,16,16});
    mesh.getConnectivity().compute(cellDim, cellDim);
    BalancedCompactPartitioner partitioner(mesh);
    partitioner.partition(world.size());
    sharder.shard(partitioner);
    sharder.scatter(0);
  }
  auto mesh = sharder.gather(0);

  // Compute distributed connectivity and reconcile shared entities
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(2, 3);
  mesh.reconcile(2);
  @endcode

  # Submodules

  - **Assembly** — MPI-parallel assembly strategies for PETSc-backed forms
  - **Context** — `Context::MPI` execution context
  - **Geometry** — Distributed mesh and sharder
  - **IO** — HDF5 and MFEM IO specializations for distributed meshes
  - **Variational** — Distributed FE spaces (P1 on MPI mesh)

  @note Requires MPI to be available and Rodin configured with
  `RODIN_USE_MPI=ON`.

  @see @ref Rodin::Context::MPI, @ref Rodin::Geometry::Shard
 */

/**
  @namespace Rodin::PETSc
  @brief PETSc storage, assembly and linear/nonlinear solver adapters.

  PETSc-backed trial/test functions connect Rodin variational forms to PETSc
  matrices, vectors, Krylov solvers and nonlinear solvers. The mesh execution
  context determines whether those objects are local or distributed.
  @ref guides-backends describes the relationship between context, assembly
  strategy and algebraic storage.

  # Object lifetime

  Initialize PETSc before constructing its objects. Destroy Rodin objects
  owning PETSc matrices, vectors and solver contexts before finalization.

  @code{.cpp}
  PetscErrorCode ierr = PetscInitialize(&argc, &argv, PETSC_NULLPTR, PETSC_NULLPTR);
  assert(ierr == PETSC_SUCCESS);
  {
    // Construct and use PETSc-backed spaces, fields, forms and solvers here.
  }
  ierr = PetscFinalize();
  assert(ierr == PETSC_SUCCESS);
  @endcode

  In distributed programs, the MPI environment and communicator must also
  outlive their borrowers. Collective operations require participation in
  the same order on all ranks. See @ref guides-mpi for partitioning,
  ownership, ghost data and collective queries.

  # Variational storage and solvers

  Construct PETSc trial and test functions on a compatible finite element space.

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 space(mesh);
  PETSc::Variational::TrialFunction u(space);
  PETSc::Variational::TestFunction v(space);
  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v)) - Integral(f, v)
          + DirichletBC(u, Zero());
  PETSc::Solver::KSP solver(poisson);
  solver.solve();
  @endcode

  This fragment assumes the mesh, right-hand side and required connectivity
  have already been prepared. It must execute within the initialized scope.
  Select KSP and preconditioner settings for the actual matrix properties and
  inspect convergence status. @ref guides-petsc contains a complete Poisson
  program and explicit command-line configuration.

  Multiple trial/test pairs represent coupled systems, including velocity,
  pressure and pressure-mean constraints for Stokes flow. The field-split
  preconditioner must use the corresponding field index sets and block
  names. Its configuration depends on the formulation and block ordering;
  see the multivariable examples linked from @ref examples-index and the
  field-split discussion in @ref guides-petsc.

  @ref Rodin::PETSc::Solver::SNES handles nonlinear residual and Jacobian
  callbacks. Their state updates, assembly and convergence criteria must be
  consistent with the nonlinear weak problem.

  # Submodules

  | Module | Role |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref Rodin::PETSc::Math | Matrix, vector and linear-system handles |
  | @ref Rodin::PETSc::Variational | PETSc-backed fields, forms and problems |
  | @ref Rodin::PETSc::Assembly | Sequential, OpenMP and distributed assembly specializations |
  | @ref Rodin::PETSc::Solver | Linear and nonlinear solver adapters |

  Configure Rodin with `RODIN_USE_PETSC=ON`; distributed execution additionally
  requires its MPI configuration. Availability of a particular solver,
  factorization or preconditioner depends on the PETSc installation.

  @see @ref guides-petsc, @ref guides-mpi, @ref guides-numerical-contracts
 */

